Package: w11
Architecture: all
Depends: ${python3:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         gir1.2-gtk-3.0,
         python3-gi
Recommends: xdotool,
            wmctrl,
            x11-utils,
            x11-xserver-utils
Suggests: wl-mirror
Description: X11 power tools as drop-in clones for Wayland
 Seven commands that behave like the X11 tools they clone -- same commands,
 same flags, same output bytes -- on a Wayland session:
 .
   * wdotool  - xdotool: key/type/click/mousemove and the window commands
   * wwmctl   - wmctrl: list and act on windows, desktops and workspaces
   * wxprop   - xprop: properties of native and XWayland windows
   * wxrandr  - xrandr: query and reshape the monitor layout
   * warandr  - arandr: the drag-your-monitors GUI, on Wayland and on X11
   * wmirror  - mirror a region or an odd-shaped output on wlroots (wl-mirror)
   * xw11     - an X11 display in front of the session's X server, so the
     ORIGINAL xdotool, wmctrl, xprop, xrandr and every other X client answer
     about the whole desktop
 .
 On an X11 session the first four hand over to the real xdotool, wmctrl, xprop
 and xrandr with execve, argv untouched, so one script runs on both session
 types.  On a Wayland session with those four installed they hand over as well,
 to the original running against xw11's display -- which is why they are
 Recommends and not Suggests: with them present the tools you already know are
 what runs, and W11_PROXY=never asks for the clones instead.  The originals are
 never replaced: these commands are installed under their own names only, and
 nothing in this package overwrites a file owned by xdotool, wmctrl, x11-utils
 or x11-xserver-utils.
 .
 Also installed: the GNOME Shell bridge extension that the window commands
 need on GNOME (system-wide, enabled for each user at their next login), a
 udev rule that lets the user at the active seat open /dev/uinput without
 root, and the warandr application-menu entry.  A second, separate GNOME
 Shell extension is carried for wxrandr --unsafe-gnome-overlap and is enabled
 for nobody: nothing in this package turns it on, and it does nothing until
 somebody enables it by hand.  See README.Debian.
